# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads .env from this directory automatically. The .NET
# services do NOT -- see "Running the services" below.
#
# Every value here is for LOCAL DEVELOPMENT. The central tier takes its
# credentials from a secret store, never from a file in the repository, the
# same rule the certificate signing key follows.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL (docker compose)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose refuses to start if the user or password is unset, rather than
# falling back to a default. A default password is a password everyone has.
#
# NOTE: PostgreSQL applies these only when it initialises an empty data
# directory. Changing them later does not change the existing database --
# recreate the volume as well:
#
#     docker compose down && docker volume rm ncbrs_ncbrs-postgres
#
POSTGRES_DB=ncbrs
POSTGRES_USER=ncbrs
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ncbrs-dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keycloak bootstrap admin (docker compose)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The admin console account, not a registrar. Previously admin/admin.
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin-dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running the services against PostgreSQL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .NET configuration does not read .env. Export these in the shell that runs
# the service, or set them in your launch profile:
#
#     Database__Provider=Postgres
#     ConnectionStrings__Default=Host=localhost;Port=5433;Database=ncbrs;Username=ncbrs;Password=ncbrs-dev
#
# Port 5433, not 5432 -- see docker-compose.yml for why.
#
# The default provider is SQLite, so none of this is needed for ordinary
# development.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is NOT here, and why
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The four development registrar accounts (nurse.banda, dr.tembo,
# district.officer, ministry.admin, all with the password "password") live in
# keycloak/ncbrs-realm.json and are committed.
#
# They are NOT moved here, because Keycloak's realm import does not substitute
# environment variables -- this was tested, and a placeholder in the realm
# file becomes the literal password. Fixture accounts whose password is the
# string "${NCBRS_DEV_USER_PASSWORD}" would look secured while being trivially
# guessable, which is worse than the honest version.
#
# The control is therefore a rule rather than a secret: **this realm is
# development fixture data and must never be imported into a Keycloak anyone
# else can reach.** Compose runs it with `start-dev` on an in-memory database
# and plain HTTP, which is not a production mode in any case.
